{"reference":"Song of Songs 8","book":"Song of Songs","chapter":8,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Love’s unquenchable force","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Verses 6–7 intensify the poem’s language for love: it is compared with death, the grave, fire, and floodwaters, and it cannot be purchased with a household’s wealth. The unusual expression translated “a very flame of Jehovah” is disputed: it may contain the divine name Yah, or it may simply heighten the image as a mighty flame. Either way, the poetry presents love as powerful and beyond monetary exchange.","verse_refs":["Song of Songs 8:6","Song of Songs 8:6-7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_song_of_songs_008_2f92abd1ace108807607a4be"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Refrain and voices","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The appeal to the daughters of Jerusalem in verse 4 repeats a refrain about the arousal or timing of love. Its exact syntax and force remain debated, so it should not be treated as a universal timetable for modern relationships. The changing voices in verses 8–9 also call for care: the poem distinguishes the woman, man, daughters of Jerusalem, brothers, and others through grammar and direct address, but many individual speaker assignments are uncertain.","verse_refs":["Song of Songs 8:4","Song of Songs 8:8-9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_song_of_songs_008_0489474d3b1cc6d274f0af1f"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading the Song as a whole","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The Song is preserved in four fragmentary Hebrew manuscripts from the Judean Desert and in a highly literal Old Greek translation called Asma asmaton. Its recurring refrains, searches, descriptions, images, and phrases give the book poetic coherence, but they do not establish one uncontested continuous plot, wedding cycle, dream sequence, or biography. Later Jewish and Christian allegorical readings are important reception traditions, yet they are not explicit identifications made by the poem’s speakers.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_song_of_songs_008_d87cbc15fc8b27c185986821"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}